Central Retinal Artery/Vein Occlusion
Opthalmology & ENT
Sudden painless vision loss, Cherry-red spot OR diffuse hemorrhages, Relative afferent pupillary defect

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-010
Reason for Visit: Sudden, profound unilateral loss of vision mimicking a stroke.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute, sudden, painless, and profound unilateral loss of vision in an elderly patient with cardiovascular risk factors. This represents an absolute ophthalmological emergency and a potential stroke equivalent. An urgent home visit was strictly required to evaluate for concomitant cerebrovascular deficits, confirm the presence of an acute ischemic retinal event, and facilitate immediate emergency escalation to a stroke/ophthalmology center to attempt visual salvage within the critical time window.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "A black curtain just dropped over my right eye ten minutes ago, and now I can't see anything at all on that side."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden, painless, strictly unilateral, profound loss of vision in the right eye occurring suddenly while watching television. Describes a dense visual field constriction that rapidly progressed to near-total darkness (scotoma) corresponding to the right eye. Denies any eye pain, redness, flashes of light, or trauma. Strong clinical history of systemic cardiovascular risk factors, including severe hypertension and hypercholesterolemia. Denies jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, or temporal headache.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently highly distressed and disoriented due to monocular vision loss.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety; requires immediate medical transport.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 185/100 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Acutely distressed, covering the right eye, visibly anxious.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity in the right eye is reduced to light perception only. Left eye is 6/9. A pronounced relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) is immediately visible in the right eye indicating severe optic nerve/retinal dysfunction. Direct ophthalmoscopy of the right eye reveals a pale, ischemic, and opaque retina with a prominent cherry-red spot visible in the fovea. Segmentation of the blood column (boxcarring) is visible within the retinal arterioles. Neurological examination reveals no facial droop, normal speech, and 5/5 motor strength in all extremities. No temporal artery tenderness.
Environmental Observations: Patient is packed and ready for transport.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient on Aspirin and Ramipril; reports missing doses recently.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Central Retinal Artery Occlusion (CRAO) - Status: Acute Emergency
Differentials:

Ischemic Stroke (Occipital Lobe) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Strictly unilateral monocular vision loss rather than a homonymous hemianopia, and the presence of a distinct cherry-red spot on retinal examination.

Giant Cell Arteritis (Temporal Arteritis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, temporal headache, and lack of systemic inflammatory symptoms, though requires formal ESR/CRP testing.

Retinal Detachment - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of RAPD, cherry-red spot, pale retina, and absence of prior floaters or photopsia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Impending permanent organ damage)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a sudden, painless, profound monocular vision loss, representing an acute 'stroke of the eye'. Urgent face-to-face assessment was critical to confirm the diagnosis of Central Retinal Artery Occlusion, evidenced by the profound RAPD, pale retina, and pathognomonic cherry-red spot. The immediate bedside evaluation decisively differentiated this from an intracranial stroke while confirming the critical nature of the ischemia. Because retinal tissue suffers irreversible damage within 90-120 minutes of complete occlusion, this rapid diagnostic confirmation mandated immediate 000 ambulance transfer to a tertiary center for urgent ocular massage, intraocular pressure reduction, and potential thrombolytic therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed immediate gentle digital ocular massage in an attempt to dislodge the embolus while awaiting the ambulance. Administered one dose of oral Aspirin 300mg STAT. Arranged immediate Code 1 Ambulance transfer to the nearest tertiary emergency department with ophthalmology cover.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent future embolic events, the patient must link medication compliance directly to vision preservation. The explicit instruction is: "Place your blood pressure medication directly on top of your glasses case every night." This forces an unavoidable physical interaction with the medication before correcting vision each morning, structurally embedding the compliance habit.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review post-hospital discharge for aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently being transported directly to the Emergency Department via ambulance. Immediate handover provided to paramedics regarding presumed CRAO and time of onset.

